From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: retire an RK3576 job on the hardware's own
 account of the kick

The RK3576 has no completion interrupt, so 0006 polls for the job being
over and 0012 makes it wait past that for the writing block's own
completion, bounded by dpu_grace_us.  Neither of the two signals that
wait is anchored on says what it was taken to say.

PC_OPERATION_ENABLE is a self-clearing GO bit.  The vendor rknpu driver
writes 1 and then 0 to it back to back (rknpu_job.c) [source-confirmed],
and on this part it reads back zero at every poll of a kick that is
still running [HW sweep, H96 MAX M9] -- so "OPERATION_ENABLE == 0", the
first half of the completion condition, has been vacuously true since
the polled path was written.  Every wait under it therefore started at
the FIRST POLL TICK rather than at an event, which is not what
dpu_grace_us was measured as and not what its name says.

PC_DONE, the other half, is raised per TASK and not per kick.  0016
made a job of n tasks go out as one hardware kick, and the two PC_DONE
bits alternate as the program counter retires each program of it.
Traced at a 10 us poll over the 35-program cross-layer kick of a
MobileNetV1-224 [HW sweep]:

  t=   12 us   PC_DONE_0 set, task 1 of 35 in flight
  t=   31 us   PC_DONE_1 set
   ...         the two alternating, once per task, for 1.9 ms
  t= 1911 us   task 35 of 35 in flight
  t= 1921 us   the DPU's own completion, and the kick is over

So dpu_grace_us, measured as one task's drain, was being asked to cover
a whole stream.  Past it the driver signals the fence while the core is
still writing: the caller reads a surface that never landed, and the
next job is programmed into a core that has not finished the last one.
From userspace that looks like a hardware bound on how long a chained
stream may be, with a magic number in a shipping path.  It is not one.

Scaling the deadline by the task count is the obvious response and is
not enough: it is a fitted number, and a stream whose programs each
take longer than dpu_grace_us outruns it.  A 224x224 k3 convolution at
256 channels row-splits into 75 chained tasks; scaled, the deadline
expires at 37.5 ms -- 75 * 500 us to the microsecond -- with the task
counter reading 67 tasks started of 75 [HW sweep].

The part says exactly where a kick is.  PC_TASK_STATUS -- the register
the vendor driver reads as its "task counter", and whose offset it
carries per SoC, 0x3c on RK3588 and 0x48 here [source-confirmed] -- is
a live pair of counters, both modulo the programmed TASK_NUMBER [HW
sweep, kicks of 2, 3, 5, 8, 9, 35 and 90 tasks]:

  bits 15:0     tasks started
  bits 31:16    tasks completed

Mid-stream at least one of the two is non-zero: the started count wraps
to zero only as the last task starts, and the completed count only once
every task has retired.  So with a PC_DONE in hand -- something has
retired -- a zero register means the whole kick is over, and nothing
else does.

So anchor the wait there: hold it off until PC_DONE says a task has
retired and, for a batched job, the counter says all of them have.
dpu_grace_us is then once again the drain it was measured as, applied
to the program that wrote last, and how long a batched stream may be
stops being a property of the driver's timing.  A job of one task
programs TASK_NUMBER = 1, where both counters read zero always, so
PC_DONE alone is its signal and its wait does not change.

This also closes a race the old condition had: the per-task block
completion bits do not persist across tasks [HW sweep], so a poll
landing while a MID-STREAM task's DPU bit was still set would retire
the job early -- rare enough never to have been caught, and
indistinguishable from a correct retire when it happened.

What bounds a job that raises no PC_DONE at all is a backstop rather
than a deadline: it retires after a quarter of the scheduler's job
timeout, with the reason logged.  It is not a tuning knob, and no job
in the gates reaches it -- 29 gates, zero backstop hits, which is also
what says every class raises PC_DONE, the pooling and LUT-load programs
included.

Measured on an H96 MAX M9 [HW sweep]:

  - the 224x224 k3 convolution above is bit-exact, 150 programs in one
    kick, where a deadline scaled by the task count retires it at
    t=37554 us and the layer computes wrong ("a row task wrote nothing
    over 8 attempts");
  - 29 gates pass, the kernel taint word unmoved at every one;
  - MobileNetV1-224 is 6.5 ms per inference in 5 submits and 40 tasks,
    and the per-submit floor is a median of 348 us: both unchanged,
    because the DPU's own completion is when the wait ends either way
    and this patch only changes when it begins.

The interface version goes to 1.4.  No uAPI changes: the bump exists
because only from here is a batched stream's length bounded by nothing
but the scheduler's job timeout, and a caller that sizes its streams
has no other way to tell.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h |  13 ++--
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c  |  11 ++-
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c  | 110 ++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 3 files changed, 103 insertions(+), 31 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
index 3811bb562..b1b8c10aa 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
@@ -41,6 +41,7 @@ struct rocket_soc_data {
 	unsigned int num_clks;		/* clk_bulk count: 4 base, 6 with CBUF */
 	unsigned int num_resets;	/* reset_bulk count: 2 base, 1 on RK3576 */
 	unsigned int task_number_bits;	/* PC_TASK_CON TASK_NUMBER width: 12 base, 16 on RK3576 */
+	unsigned int task_status_offset;/* PC_TASK_STATUS: 0x3c base, 0x48 on RK3576 */
 	bool poll_completion;		/* PC_DONE not routable to the GIC; poll it */
 };
 
@@ -84,11 +85,15 @@ struct rocket_core {
 	struct work_struct poll_work;
 	atomic_t poll_active;
 	/*
-	 * When PC_DONE was first seen for the task in flight, so the wait
-	 * for the DPU's own completion can be bounded.  Zero while none
-	 * has been seen.
+	 * When the kick was seen to be over -- every task of it retired -- so
+	 * the wait for the writing block's own completion can be bounded.  Zero
+	 * while it has not been.
 	 */
-	ktime_t poll_pc_done_at;
+	ktime_t poll_kick_over_at;
+	/* When the kick was started, for the backstop below. */
+	ktime_t poll_started_at;
+	/* How many tasks this job's single kick covers. */
+	unsigned int poll_tasks;
 	/*
 	 * How long past PC_DONE to keep waiting for that completion, in
 	 * microseconds.  Latched per submit from the job's class (see
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
index 8f1be538b..445cdf3f9 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
@@ -165,6 +165,13 @@ DEFINE_DRM_ACCEL_FOPS(rocket_accel_driver_fops);
  * - 1.1 - adds DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED (per-job chained submit).
  * - 1.2 - adds DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE (per-job completion-class hint).
  * - 1.3 - adds DRM_ROCKET_JOB_PPU_DONE (retire on the PPU's completion).
+ * - 1.4 - a job's completion wait is anchored on the hardware's own account of
+ *         where the kick is (PC_DONE plus PC_TASK_STATUS) rather than on a
+ *         deadline from the first poll, so how long a batched stream may be is
+ *         bounded by nothing but the scheduler's job timeout.  No new uAPI: the
+ *         bump exists so userspace can tell, because on an older kernel a long
+ *         batched stream can retire with programs still to run and a caller
+ *         that sizes its streams has no other way to know it may stop.
  *
  * Userspace MUST NOT self-chain a job's regcmd layout unless the driver reports
  * >= 1.1: an older kernel does not know the flag, would run that chained layout
@@ -172,7 +179,7 @@ DEFINE_DRM_ACCEL_FOPS(rocket_accel_driver_fops);
  */
 static const struct drm_driver rocket_drm_driver = {
 	.major			= 1,
-	.minor			= 3,
+	.minor			= 4,
 	.driver_features	= DRIVER_COMPUTE_ACCEL | DRIVER_GEM,
 	.open			= rocket_open,
 	.postclose		= rocket_postclose,
@@ -245,6 +252,7 @@ static const struct rocket_soc_data rk3588_soc_data = {
 	.num_clks = 4,
 	.num_resets = 2,
 	.task_number_bits = 12,
+	.task_status_offset = 0x3c,
 	.poll_completion = false,
 };
 
@@ -252,6 +260,7 @@ static const struct rocket_soc_data rk3576_soc_data = {
 	.num_clks = 6,
 	.num_resets = 1,
 	.task_number_bits = 16,
+	.task_status_offset = 0x48,
 	.poll_completion = true,
 };
 
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 3139f09a3..dbb5de586 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -82,6 +82,57 @@ static inline ktime_t rocket_poll_period(void)
 	return ns_to_ktime((s64)us * NSEC_PER_USEC);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The safety valve, from the kick, for a job that raises no PC_DONE at all: it
+ * still retires well before the scheduler's own timeout takes the core down.
+ * Deliberately far longer than any wait below -- it is not a tuning knob, and
+ * a job reaching it has already gone wrong.
+ */
+#define ROCKET_POLL_BACKSTOP_US (JOB_TIMEOUT_MS * USEC_PER_MSEC / 4)
+
+/*
+ * Is this kick over -- every task of it retired?
+ *
+ * PC_DONE is raised per TASK, not per kick.  On a batched job the two PC_DONE
+ * bits alternate as the program counter retires each program, so the first is
+ * seen a few tens of microseconds into a stream that may run for milliseconds,
+ * and it says nothing about the stream being over.
+ *
+ * PC_TASK_STATUS does.  It is a live pair of counters, both modulo the
+ * programmed TASK_NUMBER [HW sweep, H96 MAX M9]:
+ *
+ *   bits 15:0     tasks started
+ *   bits 31:16    tasks completed
+ *
+ * Mid-stream at least one of the two is non-zero -- the started count only
+ * wraps to zero as the LAST task starts, and the completed count only wraps
+ * once every task has retired -- so with a PC_DONE in hand (something has
+ * retired) a zero register means the whole kick is over, and nothing else does.
+ * A job of one task programs TASK_NUMBER = 1, where both counters are zero
+ * always, so PC_DONE alone is its signal and its behaviour does not change.
+ *
+ * (The vendor rknpu driver reads the same register as its "task counter" and
+ * carries its offset per SoC, 0x3c on RK3588 and 0x48 here [source-confirmed].)
+ *
+ * PC_OPERATION_ENABLE is NOT part of this test on the polled path: OP_EN is a
+ * self-clearing GO bit -- the vendor driver writes 1 then 0 to it back to back
+ * [source-confirmed] -- and it reads back zero at every poll of a kick that is
+ * still running [HW sweep].  Left in the completion condition it is vacuously
+ * true, which is what made every wait below start at the first poll tick
+ * instead of at an event.
+ */
+static bool rocket_poll_kick_over(struct rocket_core *core, u32 raw)
+{
+	if (!(raw & (PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_RK3576_PC_DONE_0 |
+		     PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_RK3576_PC_DONE_1)))
+		return false;
+
+	if (core->poll_tasks <= 1)
+		return true;
+
+	return readl(core->pc_iomem + core->soc->task_status_offset) == 0;
+}
+
 /*
  * PC_TASK_CON packs TASK_NUMBER in the low bits with the control bits directly
  * above it: TASK_PP_EN, then TASK_COUNT_CLEAR, then a bit the TRM marks
@@ -306,10 +357,12 @@ static void rocket_job_hw_submit(struct rocket_core *core, struct rocket_job *jo
 	rocket_pc_writel(core, OPERATION_ENABLE, PC_OPERATION_ENABLE_OP_EN(1));
 
 	if (core->soc->poll_completion) {
-		core->poll_pc_done_at = 0;
+		core->poll_kick_over_at = 0;
+		core->poll_started_at = ktime_get();
+		core->poll_tasks = job->batched ? job->task_count : 1;
 		/*
-		 * Latch the deadline for THIS job before the timer can fire, so
-		 * the poll callback never needs to reach for the job.
+		 * Latch the grace for THIS job before the timer can fire, so the
+		 * poll callback never needs to reach for the job.
 		 */
 		core->poll_grace_us = job->no_dpu_done
 			? READ_ONCE(rocket_dpu_blind_us)
@@ -583,41 +636,46 @@ static enum hrtimer_restart rocket_poll_timer_fn(struct hrtimer *timer)
 		return HRTIMER_NORESTART;
 
 	/*
-	 * On RK3576, OPERATION_ENABLE is not cleared by hardware on completion;
-	 * check INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS bits 28-29 (PC_DONE_0/1) instead.
+	 * On RK3576 there is no completion interrupt, so the kick is followed in
+	 * INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS: PC_DONE (bits 28-29) per task, and the writing
+	 * block's own completion once the last of them has retired.
+	 *
+	 * The kick being over is not the surface being readable.  PC_DONE says
+	 * the program counter finished issuing, not that the write DMA has
+	 * drained; the writing block raises its own completion -- the bit RK3588
+	 * takes as its interrupt -- tens to hundreds of microseconds later, and a
+	 * fence signalled before that hands the caller a surface whose tail has
+	 * not landed.  WHICH block writes is the job's class: the DPU for a
+	 * convolution or a matmul, the PPU for a pooling program, which enables
+	 * no DPU stage at all.
+	 *
+	 * That wait is bounded by the grace, so a task raising no completion at
+	 * all cannot stall behind one -- and because it now starts when the kick
+	 * is over rather than at the first poll, the grace is once again the
+	 * drain it was measured as.
 	 */
 	raw = rocket_pc_readl(core, INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS);
-	if (rocket_pc_readl(core, OPERATION_ENABLE) == 0 ||
-	    (raw & (PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_RK3576_PC_DONE_0 |
-		    PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_RK3576_PC_DONE_1))) {
-		/*
-		 * PC_DONE says the program counter finished issuing the task, not
-		 * that the writing block's DMA has drained.  That block raises its
-		 * own completion -- the bit RK3588 takes as its interrupt -- tens
-		 * of microseconds later, and a fence signalled on PC_DONE hands
-		 * the caller a surface whose tail has not landed yet.  Wait for
-		 * it.  WHICH block writes is the job's class: the DPU for a
-		 * convolution or a matmul, the PPU for a pooling program, which
-		 * enables no DPU stage at all.
-		 *
-		 * Bounded, so a task that raises no completion at all cannot
-		 * stall behind one: after the grace the job retires on PC_DONE
-		 * as it did before.
-		 */
+	if (rocket_poll_kick_over(core, raw)) {
 		if (raw & core->poll_done_mask)
 			goto complete;
-		if (!core->poll_pc_done_at)
-			core->poll_pc_done_at = ktime_get();
-		else if (ktime_us_delta(ktime_get(), core->poll_pc_done_at) >=
+		if (!core->poll_kick_over_at)
+			core->poll_kick_over_at = ktime_get();
+		else if (ktime_us_delta(ktime_get(), core->poll_kick_over_at) >=
 			 (s64)core->poll_grace_us)
 			goto complete;
+	} else if (ktime_us_delta(ktime_get(), core->poll_started_at) >=
+		   (s64)ROCKET_POLL_BACKSTOP_US) {
+		dev_err(core->dev,
+			"job of %u task(s) raised no PC_DONE in %u us; retiring it\n",
+			core->poll_tasks, (unsigned int)ROCKET_POLL_BACKSTOP_US);
+		goto complete;
 	}
 
 	hrtimer_forward_now(timer, rocket_poll_period());
 	return HRTIMER_RESTART;
 
 complete:
-	core->poll_pc_done_at = 0;
+	core->poll_kick_over_at = 0;
 	atomic_set(&core->poll_active, 0);
 	schedule_work(&core->poll_work);
 	return HRTIMER_NORESTART;
-- 
2.43.0

